This is a wrapper around libigl to use in C#.
The wrapper is significantly underdeveloped, but
is simply used for winding number computations.

The project has two build stages:

  1. A C++ shared library (libigl_core.dll / .dylib / .so) that wraps libigl
     functions, built with CMake.

  2. A C# class library (wrapper.dll) that P/Invokes into libigl_core,
     built with the .NET SDK.

The C++ code is cross-platform. The same CMakeLists.txt and source files
build on Windows (MSVC), macOS (clang++), and Linux (g++ or clang++).


== Source layout ==

  eigen/                  Eigen header library (version 3.4.0 recommended)
  src/
    libigl/               libigl header library
      include/igl/        igl headers
    LibIGLWrapper.cpp     exported C functions (winding number, dot product)
    LibIGLWrapper.h       platform export macros, SAL annotation stubs
    UnixSal.h             SAL no-ops for non-Windows builds
    CMakeLists.txt        native library build
    x64_lib/              native library output (created by build_native.bat/.sh)
      libigl_core.dll     Windows
      libigl_core.dylib   macOS
      libigl_core.so      Linux
  wrapper/
    core/
      EigenBasic.cs       [DllImport] P/Invoke declarations
      EigenMethods.cs     safe managed wrappers (EigenDenseUtilities)
    bin/Release/net7.0/   C# library output (created by dotnet build)
      wrapper.dll         the managed wrapper assembly
      wrapper.pdb         debug symbols
    wrapper.csproj        .NET 7 class library


== Known-good libigl version ==

  Commit: 1c8c6d38bad6ad4b962dce3d5f54834c8c46f5a2  (2023-10-06, Alec Jacobson)

  Clone into src/libigl/:
      git clone https://github.com/libigl/libigl src/libigl
      cd src/libigl && git checkout 1c8c6d38bad6ad4b962dce3d5f54834c8c46f5a2


════════════════════════════════════════
  WINDOWS (VS Code + MSVC)
════════════════════════════════════════

Prerequisites
  - Visual Studio Build Tools (any recent version)
      winget install Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.BuildTools
      Select the "Desktop development with C++" workload.
      Ninja is included automatically with this workload.
  - CMake 3.10+
      winget install Kitware.CMake
  - .NET 7 SDK or later
      winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.7

Step 1 — Build the native DLL
  From the libigl_wrapper/ directory in any terminal (the script loads
  the MSVC environment itself):

      build_native.bat

  Output: src/x64_lib/libigl_core.dll

Step 2 — Build the C# wrapper
      dotnet build wrapper/wrapper.csproj -c Release

  Output: wrapper/bin/Release/net7.0/wrapper.dll

VS Code tip
  Open a new terminal with Ctrl+` — it opens as a Developer Command Prompt
  (configured in settings.json), so cl.exe and cmake are on the PATH.
  You can also run both build steps directly from the VS Code terminal.


════════════════════════════════════════
  macOS (VS Code + Xcode Command Line Tools)
════════════════════════════════════════

Prerequisites
  - Xcode Command Line Tools (provides clang++)
      xcode-select --install
  - Homebrew  (https://brew.sh)
  - CMake 3.10+ and Ninja
      brew install cmake ninja
  - .NET 7 SDK or later
      brew install --cask dotnet-sdk
      # or download from https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download

Step 1 — Build the native dylib
  From the libigl_wrapper/ directory:

      chmod +x build_native.sh
      ./build_native.sh

  Output: src/x64_lib/libigl_core.dylib

Step 2 — Build the C# wrapper
      dotnet build wrapper/wrapper.csproj -c Release

  Output: wrapper/bin/Release/net7.0/wrapper.dll

VS Code tip
  Install the "C/C++" and "C# Dev Kit" extensions. The integrated terminal
  already has clang++ on the PATH after installing Command Line Tools.
  CMake Tools extension can optionally configure and build the native
  library from the IDE (point it at src/CMakeLists.txt).


════════════════════════════════════════
  LINUX (CMake + .NET SDK)
════════════════════════════════════════

Prerequisites (Ubuntu / Debian)
      sudo apt update
      sudo apt install build-essential cmake ninja-build

  .NET 7 SDK or later — follow the official instructions for your distro:
      https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/install/linux

  For other distros substitute the appropriate package manager (dnf, pacman,
  zypper, etc.) and package names (gcc-c++, cmake, etc.).

Step 1 — Build the native .so
  From the libigl_wrapper/ directory:

      chmod +x build_native.sh
      ./build_native.sh

  Output: src/x64_lib/libigl_core.so

Step 2 — Build the C# wrapper
      dotnet build wrapper/wrapper.csproj -c Release

  Output: wrapper/bin/Release/net7.0/wrapper.dll

To rebuild from scratch on any Unix platform, delete src/build_unix/ and
re-run build_native.sh.


════════════════════════════════════════
  HOW THE BUILD WORKS
════════════════════════════════════════

Native library (src/CMakeLists.txt)
  CMake compiles LibIGLWrapper.cpp as a shared library named libigl_core
  (CMAKE_SHARED_LIBRARY_PREFIX "" removes the default "lib" prefix on Unix
  so the name matches what [DllImport("libigl_core")] expects on all
  platforms).

  Both build_native.bat (Windows) and build_native.sh (macOS/Linux) use
  -G Ninja so all platforms get parallel incremental builds via the same
  generator. On Windows, Ninja ships with the VS Build Tools C++ workload
  and is added to PATH by vcvarsall.bat.

  Three include paths are set:
    ../eigen/             Eigen matrix library headers
    .                     so #include "libigl/include/igl/..." resolves
    ./libigl/include      so igl headers can #include <igl/...> internally

  LibIGLWrapper.h selects the correct export macro per platform:
    Windows:  __declspec(dllexport)
    Unix:     __attribute__((visibility("default")))

C# wrapper (wrapper/)
  EigenBasic.cs declares [DllImport("libigl_core")] P/Invoke bindings.
  .NET resolves the library name to:
    Windows:  libigl_core.dll
    macOS:    libigl_core.dylib
    Linux:    libigl_core.so

  EigenMethods.cs (EigenDenseUtilities) provides the public managed API:

    Dot(ReadOnlySpan<double>, ReadOnlySpan<double>, int) -> double
        Eigen vector dot product.

    WindingNumber(verts, n_verts, vert_dim,
                  faces, n_faces, face_dim,
                  points, n_points, point_dim,
                  winding_numbers)
        igl::winding_number — batch winding numbers for query points
        against a triangle mesh.

    WindingNumberSlow(verts, n_verts, vert_dim,
                      faces, n_faces, face_dim,
                      point, point_dim) -> double
        Single-point variant of WindingNumber.

  All arrays are flat, row-major. Vertex/face/point data must be
  pre-allocated by the caller; winding_numbers is written in-place.


════════════════════════════════════════
  USAGE EXAMPLE
════════════════════════════════════════

  Add a reference to wrapper.dll and ensure libigl_core.dll/.dylib/.so
  sits in the same directory at runtime:

      dotnet add reference path/to/wrapper.dll

  using EigenWrapper.Eigen;

  // Flat row-major arrays: N vertices (Nx3), M triangles (Mx3), P queries (Px3)
  double[] verts  = ...;
  int[]    faces  = ...;
  double[] points = ...;
  double[] wind   = new double[P];

  EigenDenseUtilities.WindingNumber(
      verts,  N, 3,
      faces,  M, 3,
      points, P, 3,
      wind);
